## 1. Modular Resonance and the Filtration Hierarchy

To formalize the framework, we begin by defining a sequence of modular filters derived from the primorials, denoted as \( M_k = \prod_{i=1}^k p_i \), where \( p_i \) is the \( i \)th prime number. Each \( M_k \) constitutes a harmonic base that defines a recursive residue space in which prime pairs \( (n, n + 2) \) can be tested for modular coherence.

A candidate twin prime pair \( (n, n + 2) \) is said to **survive** a modular filter \( M_k \) if neither \( n \mod p_i \) nor \( n + 2 \mod p_i \) is zero for any \( p_i \mid M_k \). We define the binary indicator function:

$$
\Phi(n, M_k) =
\begin{cases}
1 & \text{if } \gcd(n(n+2), M_k) = 1 \\
0 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
$$

This function operates as a harmonic sieve, isolating pairs \( n \) that remain phase-compatible with all primes embedded in \( M_k \). The recursive application of \( \Phi(n, M_k) \) across increasingly large values of \( k \) results in a **resonant decay profile** that is logarithmically stable — i.e., survival rates decrease predictably and converge toward a nonzero asymptote.

We define the **twin echo field** over a domain \( N \) as:

$$
E_{M_k}(N) = \sum_{n = 2}^{N} \Phi(n, M_k)
$$

The normalized survival ratio, \( R(M_k) = \frac{E_{M_k}(N)}{N} \), provides a scalar measure of twin prime survivability within each harmonic shell. This metric becomes the central analytic tool for probing the persistence of twin primes as a recursive, structure-preserving function of \( k \).

In the subsequent sections, we will present empirical data demonstrating this decay behavior and visualizations of the echo fields themselves, drawing a clear connection between harmonic modular filtering and the probabilistic density of twin primes in the large-\( n \) limit."
